      explanation: 'The steepest slope on a population growth graph represents the fastest rate of increase. In an S-curve (logistic growth), the population grows fastest during the middle exponential phase, when resources are still available and the population is large enough to produce many offspring.',
      diveDeep: 'Logistic growth (S-curve) has three phases: (1) lag phase — slow initial growth as the population establishes; (2) exponential phase — rapid growth, steepest slope, maximum growth rate; (3) stationary/plateau phase — growth rate decreases as population approaches carrying capacity (K). The growth RATE is highest in the middle (exponential) phase, even though the total population is highest at the plateau. At carrying capacity, birth rate = death rate, so net growth = 0 (not "growing fastest" as choice C would suggest). Students often confuse population size (which is highest at K) with growth rate (which is highest during exponential phase). The inflection point of the S-curve (where slope is steepest) is where growth rate is maximum.',
